 This resolution rule is execution behaviour, not commentary: before it, a run had no stated way to resolve the shared `contract_id` and two runs could resolve it differently. `CHANGE_FLOW_SPECIALIZATION_REVISION` is incremented to 3.2.6 for it, so the two behaviours do not advertise the same specialization identity. `SF10-07` then scoped two body obligations to the writers that owe them, on `exact_producer_set`, `rescope_contract` and `member_registry` evidence checked against the prompt bodies: active addenda to ten, exempting all four CF Specification authors; authoring context to twelve, exempting only the two initial authors, because CF-C-40 and CF-E-40 declare one and satisfy the check. The current-PF10 obligation remains on all fourteen. That changes validation behaviour and the bundled contract, so `CHANGE_FLOW_SPECIALIZATION_REVISION` is incremented to 3.2.7. `SF10-10` then exempted the same four CF Specification authors from the current-PF10 obligation as well, on the Product Owner's ruling of 2026-09-21 that initial authoring does not require a PF10 built in an addendum and that PF10 "should not be a named input or output for an authoring prompt" -- so that roster becomes the ten non-CF writers, matching `active_addenda_required`, while membership in the fourteen-writer set and every other obligation are unchanged. That changes the bundled contract again, so `CHANGE_FLOW_SPECIALIZATION_REVISION` is incremented to 3.2.8.
 
